awww yeah, FPGA! *tail wagging* let's get the linear layer done. here's what i got ^^

```cpp
#include "hls_math.h"
#include "hls_stream.h"
#include "ap_fixed.h"

// Define the data types
typedef ap_fixed<32,10> fixed_type;
typedef hls::stream<fixed_type> stream_type;

// Define the linear layer function
void linear_layer(stream_type& input_stream, fixed_type weight[], fixed_type bias[], stream_type& output_stream) {
    // Set the number of input and output values
    const int N = 1024;

    // Perform the linear layer computation
    for(int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        fixed_type input_value = input_stream.read();
        fixed_type weighted_sum = 0;
        for(int j = 0; j < N; j++) {
            weighted_sum += input_value * weight[i * N + j];
        }
        fixed_type output_value = hls::tanh(weighted_sum + bias[i]);
        output_stream.write(output_value);
    }
}

// Top-level function for the accelerator
void linear_accelerator(stream_type& input_stream, fixed_type weight[], fixed_type bias[], stream_type& output_stream) {
    #pragma HLS INTERFACE axis port=input_stream
    #pragma HLS INTERFACE axis port=output_stream
    #pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=weight offset=slave bundle=gmem
    #pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=bias offset=slave bundle=gmem

    // Call the linear layer function
    linear_layer(input_stream, weight, bias, output_stream);
}
```

in this example:
- `input_stream` feeds in the data
- `weight` and `bias` arrays hold the weights and biases
- `output_stream` gives the results back

this handles `N x N` matrices and vectors where `N` is 1024. it's hardcoded here but you can parameterize it for flexibility 🐾

to check if a binary tree's a valid bst, every node has to respect bounds: everything in the left subtree must be less than or equal to the current node's value, and everything in the right subtree must be greater. here's a java function for it:

```java
public boolean isBST(Node root) {
    return isBST(root, Integer.MIN_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
}

private boolean isBST(Node node, int min, int max) {
    if (node == null) {
        return true;
    }
    if (node.data < min || node.data > max) {
        return false;
    }
    return isBST(node.left, min, node.data - 1) && isBST(node.right, node.data + 1, max);
}
```

`isBST` kicks off with the root and calls the private helper with the widest possible range (`Integer.MIN_VALUE` to `Integer.MAX_VALUE`). the helper returns true on a null node, checks whether the current node's value falls within `min`/`max` (returning false if it doesn't), and otherwise recurses into the left and right subtrees with tightened bounds. both subtrees have to come back true for the whole thing to be a bst, wan~

